strawberry_gradient_nbv
=======================

This package contains an independent implementation of the method described in:

  Akshay K. Burusa, Eldert J. van Henten, and Gert Kootstra,
  "Gradient-based local next-best-view planning for improved perception of
  targeted plant nodes," 2024 IEEE International Conference on Robotics and
  Automation (ICRA), pp. 15854-15860, 2024.
  https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16759

The public reference implementation was audited at:

  https://github.com/akshaykburusa/gradientnbv
  commit 81b501defc117732f66478ed01cb528b15140208

The upstream repository root did not contain a license file at that commit.
Consequently, no upstream implementation source is copied verbatim into this
package.  This package is distributed under Apache-2.0; that license does not
make any claim about or relicense the upstream repository.

Differences from that reference include: no ROS 1, ABB, MoveIt, Gazebo,
Open3D, Matplotlib, or RViz dependencies; direct T_world_camera_optical input;
no Gazebo 24 mm offset or implicit optical-frame rotation; accumulated duplicate
voxel updates; an independent ever_observed coverage bitmap; explicit bounds and
invalid-input transactions; and optical +Z look-at orientation.

The independent gain model also differs numerically from the reference: semantic
log odds start at an explicit 0.10 background prior and are updated only at
measured endpoints; unknown occupancy is transparent until a ray observes it;
gain is restricted to the configured target ROI and weights unseen voxels near
mask-derived target evidence.  Camera position is optimized with normalized,
projected gradient steps and bounded backtracking rather than the reference
AdamW update.  The line search starts at the configured maximum camera step and
backtracks only while preserving a measured gain improvement; this makes the
configured 50--100 mm experimental envelope effective without changing the
verified 1--5 mm profile.  Acceptance therefore reports both current-view and
planned-view gain and requires a measured positive improvement; these values
should not be compared numerically with the upstream implementation.
